# Line endings are not part of any contract.
#
# This repo is a CRLF checkout (core.autocrlf=true) with no .gitattributes, so
# what a test read from disk depended on the machine it ran on. A grep-style
# assertion that sliced on an LF-only literal (`"} else {\n        stats..."`)
# silently matched -1 here and sliced to EOF, swallowing the very call it
# asserted was absent -- a net-new test that was green for its author and red on
# a clean clone.
#
# Normalize in the repository, and check out platform-native.
* text=auto

# Anything executed as a POSIX script must keep LF even on Windows.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# CF-CHRONIC-REDS-CRLF (2026-09-03). `* text=auto` above normalizes what is
# STORED, but still checks out platform-native -- CRLF on Windows. A whole
# class of tests reads a source file off disk and slices it on an LF literal
# (`EMIT.indexOf("\n}\n")` in d38.addHoldingEmitCarriesBasis). On a Windows
# clone that anchor is `\r\n}\r\n`, indexOf returns -1, and the suite fails at
# FILE SCOPE before a single assertion runs -- green for a WSL/CI author, red
# on a fresh Windows clone. The 2026-08 note above diagnosed this exact shape
# and normalized storage; it did not pin the CHECKOUT, so the reds came back.
#
# Pin the checkout for the files those tests read. This is checkout
# portability, not a test change: no assertion moves.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf

# CF-CHRONIC-REDS-CRLF, same shape one level down (2026-09-04). The bcp golden
# pin compares the scraper's OWN output -- built in memory with "\n" joins --
# against a checked-in file, byte for byte. Checked out CRLF that comparison
# fails on every line on a Windows clone and passes in CI, which is the exact
# green-for-the-author/red-on-a-clean-clone shape the note above describes.
# The saved HTML fixtures are compared the same way through the emitted CSV.
*.tsv text eol=lf
backend/tests/fixtures/bcp/*.html text eol=lf
